# Official logos and vendor icons in SVG
Source: `up:stack-diagram` (caru-ini/stack-diagram, MIT), commit `f6c5785`, vendored
unchanged under `../repos/stack-diagram/`. `../UPSTREAM.stack-diagram.json` records each
file's hash measured from the fetched original. The vendored `SKILL.md` is provenance,
not a second entry point; this file is how jstack uses it.
## When to use
Use official marks when the reader recognizes the system by its products: a tech-stack
overview, a cloud architecture (AWS, Google Cloud, Azure, Kubernetes, CNCF, Databricks),
or any architecture where the user names the technologies. Otherwise keep text-only
nodes or the monochrome primitive icons of the diagram-design library. Do not mix
the two icon families in one diagram.
A logo never carries meaning alone. The node name is always written, so the grayscale
legibility rule in `output-spec.md` still holds and a missing image loses no content.
In `procurement` context logos are optional decoration; requirement coverage, the
effective 10pt floor and the omission test are unchanged.
## Fetch the marks
`fetch_icons.py` needs Python 3.9+ standard library and network access to the icon
sources. Run it into a working directory outside the skill:
```sh
python3 ${JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/diagram/repos/stack-diagram/skills/stack-diagram/scripts/fetch_icons.py icons hono "nextjs" "aws:aws/AWS-Cloud-logo" "s3:aws/s3" "queue:lucide/inbox"
```
Spec forms, sources and vendor aliases are in the vendored `SKILL.md`, Step 2. The
script writes `icons/<name>.<ext>`, an optional `<name>_dark.<ext>`, and
`icons/manifest.json` with each mark's `source` and `url`. It exits non-zero when a
mark is missing or a name is invalid; read the `FAIL` lines.
If the network or a source is unavailable, report which marks failed and draw those
nodes text-only. Never draw a lookalike, trace a logo, or substitute an emoji.
## Rules carried over unchanged
- **Never recolor a mark.** A white or near-white mark goes on a dark plate (below);
the manifest's `white_fill` flag is a heuristic, so inspect the SVG before using it.
- **Official marks only.** simple-icons is not a brand-logo source: its glyphs are
monochrome redraws. When every source fails, pass the official file as `name=URL`
from the project's own repository or site.
- **Abstract concepts get Lucide pictograms** (`name:lucide/<icon>`): queue, cron,
cache, webhook. Tinting Lucide and the bundled `assets/generic/` glyphs is allowed
because they are not brand marks.
- **Vendor sub-products without their own mark** reuse the vendor mark; the node name
carries the distinction (`Cloudflare D1`).
- **A vendor's drawing rules apply only inside that vendor's group.** AWS, Google
Cloud, Azure and Kubernetes conventions stop at the group border.
- **Credit the sources actually used** in `<desc>` or a footer, from each manifest
entry's `source` (for example `icons: svgl, devicon, AWS Architecture Icons`).
## Embed the marks
Reference each mark as a token in an `<image>` element, then inline every token as a
data URI with the vendored builder. It works on SVG as well as HTML and fails when a
token stays unresolved.
```svg
<image href="%%ICON_hono%%" x="280" y="130" width="40" height="40"
preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/>
```
```sh
python3 ${JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/diagram/repos/stack-diagram/skills/stack-diagram/scripts/build.py draft.svg icons diagram.svg
```
Never paste a downloaded mark's markup inline. It is untrusted content; inline it
could run script, and its ids and styles would collide with the diagram's. Inside
`<image>` the browser renders it as an isolated picture. Never hotlink a mark either:
the bundled `self_check.py` rejects a remote `<image>` reference, and an offline reader
would lose the logo.
## Node geometry: the card is the node
Upstream draws a 60px logo tile with the name and role below it and treats the tile
**plus** its text as one connection boundary, so no edge runs through a label. In SVG
that boundary is drawn as the node itself: a 132×104 rounded card holding the mark at
the top and the text below. The local validators then enforce what upstream left to
inspection. `verify-connectors.js --strict-rules` reports an edge that crosses another
card (rule 5), including its label area, and `verify-text-width.js` measures both
labels against the card.
| Part | Position (card top-left `x, y`, center `cx = x + 66`) |
|---|---|
| Card | `x, y`, 132 wide, 104 high, white fill, light border |
| Mark | 40×40 centered at `cx`, top at `y + 10` |
| Name | 13–14px at `y + 72`, `text-anchor="middle"` |
| Role | at least 12px at `y + 90`; upstream's 10px mono is below the local floor |
Widen the card for long or Korean names; measure them with `scripts/measure_label.js`
rather than counting characters. For a white mark, draw a dark rounded plate as a
`<path>` behind the `<image>`. Do not use a `<rect>`: a smaller rect inside the card
would become the innermost box and hide the card from the validators.
Edges attach to card sides, never to the mark. Horizontal edges meet card sides at
mid-height of the mark; vertical edges run from one card's bottom to the next card's
top; elbows run their vertical leg in a corridor free of cards and their horizontal leg
in the clear band between rows. Leave 4–6px between a line end and the card; the
arrowhead supplies the touch. Connector rules 0–6 apply unchanged.
## Spacing and groups
- Cards in one row: centers at least 140px apart. Rows: card tops 140px apart.
- A group box surrounds cards that share a runtime, vendor or repository. Its sides sit
24px outside the outermost cards, equal on both sides, measured from the card, never
from the mark.
- The group's top edge sits 48px above the first card row. The label sits inside the
top-left corner of that band, 12px from both borders, with no background, so the
border stays unbroken. Keep edges out of that corner.
- The bottom edge sits at least 24px below the last card.
- A nested group is inset at least 24px from its parent on the sides and bottom and
starts below the parent's label band. Nest one level; deeper nesting gets noisy.
- A group-label mark is a square glyph at 14px. A wordmark turns to mud at that size;
fetch the brand's square mark separately.
## Upstream rules not imported
| Upstream rule | Why not |
|---|---|
| Dashed edges mean dev-time or deploy-time relationships | `connector-rules.md` already defines dashed as transit, not interaction; one stroke cannot carry two meanings. State dev or deploy relationships in the edge label and legend |
| One accent-colored solid path marks the primary flow | Color alone fails the grayscale legibility rule; mark the primary flow with weight or labels as well |
| One arrowhead marker painted with `context-stroke` | SVG 2 paint; the Office export procedure normalizes to SVG 1.1 |
| Tiles with text below on bare canvas | The label area is invisible to the validators; the card makes it a checked box |
| Step 7, posting to the public gallery | `scripts/share.py` is not shipped; see `delivery-formats.md` before publishing anywhere |
## Checks
Run the bundled `self_check.py` and the local validators listed in `SKILL.md`, Phase 3,
on the built file, not the token draft. At delivery, add two items to visual review:
every mark is visible on its ground (white on white is the classic failure), and every
group-label mark is legible.
## HTML, PNG and Office
SVG stays the default. When the user asks for HTML or a PNG in upstream's style, copy
`../repos/stack-diagram/skills/stack-diagram/assets/template.html` into the working
directory and edit the copy. Its font stack names Japanese faces only; for Korean
labels set `lang="ko"` and replace both font tokens with `"Geist", "Noto Sans KR",
"Apple SD Gothic Neo", "Malgun Gothic", ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif`. Its
`render_png.py` needs Playwright and Chromium; follow the user's browser automation
policy instead of installing them.
Whether PowerPoint or Hangul renders an `<image>` holding a data-URI SVG has not been
verified. After importing, look at every mark; if one disappears, deliver the PNG
export from `SKILL.md`, Phase 4, and report the substitution.
